 "transcript": {
  "answer": "**Probably not just you nationally, but Perth's electricity prices haven't risen much.** Perth's electricity prices are up only about 2.4% over the year to August 2026, while the national figure is up 13.2%.\n\n- **Perth (CPI electricity, year to Aug 2026):** +2.4%. The index was 102.44 in August 2026, versus 95.18 in June 2024.\n- **Australia (CPI electricity, year to Aug 2026):** +13.2%. The index was 113.69 in August 2026.\n\nPerth's monthly figures swing a lot because of one-off state rebates being applied to bills, so single months can look misleading. Going by the recent trend, Perth's price rises have been much smaller than the rest of the country's.\n\nIf your bill feels high, the cause is more likely something specific to your household than a price rise in Perth:\n\n- **More usage:** a hot summer, heating, or an air conditioner or pool pump running more.\n- **A longer billing period:** some bills cover more than a month.\n- **A rebate ending or being smaller:** check whether a credit appeared on last year's bill that isn't on this one.\n- **A change in your plan or tariff:** look for a new supply charge or a different tariff.\n\nTo check, compare this bill's kWh usage and daily supply charge with the same period last year, or with your previous bill. If the kWh is similar but the dollar amount is higher, it's a price or plan change. If the kWh is higher, it's your usage.",
